#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231376 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f56e312-a8c4-5c65-9893-3ebc38120b4d" :knowledge-point/title "Distinguishing Between Max/Min and Argmax/Argmin"}] :question/id #uuid "6308dcce-94b4-57ff-9d03-a184ad2048bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-22126" :question/problem "Let $S=\\{-1,0,2,4\\}$ and suppose\n\n$$\nf(-1)=3,\\qquad f(0)=-2,\\qquad f(2)=5,\\qquad f(4)=1.\n$$\n\nCompute the following:\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\min_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the largest output value, while\n\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that largest output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- $\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the smallest output value, while\n\n- $\\arg\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that smallest output occurs.\n\nThe outputs of $f$ on $S$ are\n\n$$\n3,\\qquad -2,\\qquad 5,\\qquad 1.\n$$\n\nThe largest output is $5,$ and it occurs at $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=5,\n\\qquad\n\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the smallest output is $-2,$ and it occurs at $x=0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=-2,\n\\qquad\n\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "258ff70f-5f41-5ea6-a0c2-bf74e7ce1cff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Argmax and Argmin With Ties"}] :question/id #uuid "b8e4dcaf-38c1-5244-9bca-2e0eab32c34c" :question/math-academy-id "e-22127" :question/problem "Let $S=\\{-1,0,2,3\\}$ and suppose\n\n$$\nf(-1)=4,\\qquad f(0)=1,\\qquad f(2)=4,\\qquad f(3)=2.\n$$\n\nCompute $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ and $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the largest output value, while\n\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that largest output occurs.\n\nThe output values are $4,1,4,$ and $2.$ The maximum output value is $4.$\n\nThis maximum occurs at both $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{4}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\{-1,2\\}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231378 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24771a14-af10-5bc8-94d4-17c28b180c84" :knowledge-point/title "Writing and Reading Formal Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "c901e488-7c50-5af5-85b2-ce70b1447a8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-22128" :question/problem "Let $S$ be a set and let $f$ be a real-valued function on $S.$ Consider the expression\n\n$$\nx^*=\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x).\n$$\n\nWhat does this statement mean in words? Also, what does $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ represent?" :question/worked-solution "The expression $x^*=\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ means that:\n\n- $x^*$ is an input value in $S.$\n\n- Among all $x \\in S,$ the value $f(x^*)$ is as large as possible.\n\nIn other words, $x^*$ is a choice of input where $f$ attains its largest value on $S.$\n\nBy contrast, $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is not an input. It is the largest *function value* that $f$ attains on $S.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "258ff70f-5f41-5ea6-a0c2-bf74e7ce1cff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Argmax and Argmin With Ties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31464fc0-268d-513c-a882-b9f9b02a994e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533258} {:answer/id #uuid "71a394aa-8e3b-524c-8e76-c656e0db6806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.typ